# Composition Over Inheritance

Build behavior by composing small, focused types rather than growing class hierarchies.

## Rules

- Prefer protocols and protocol extensions for shared behavior instead of base classes.
- Compose capabilities from small types with constructor-injected collaborators; see [dependency-injection.mdc](.cursor/rules/dependency-injection.mdc).
- Favor value types (`struct`, `enum`) unless reference semantics are required.
- Use class inheritance only for framework superclass requirements (for example, `NSObject`). Delegate protocols are fine; keep behavior composed behind them.
- Avoid deep inheritance chains; if a subclass overrides more than a few methods, refactor toward composition.

## Examples

```swift
// ❌ BAD — behavior locked in a base class hierarchy
class BaseMeasurementEncoder {
    func encode() -> Data { fatalError("override") }
}
final class CSCSMeasurementEncoder: BaseMeasurementEncoder {
    override func encode() -> Data { /* ... */ }
}

// ✅ GOOD — protocol + composed collaborator
protocol MeasurementEncoding {
    func encode() -> Data
}
struct CSCSMeasurementEncoder: MeasurementEncoding {
    private let wheelRevolutionSource: WheelRevolutionSource
    func encode() -> Data { /* use wheelRevolutionSource */ }
}
```

Wire composed types at the composition root.
